# :substitute typed from normal mode (change.txt *:s*).

=== :s replaces the first match in the line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:  one tw|o one two
keys:   :s/two/2/<CR>
expect: |one 2 one two
mode:   normal

=== :s with g replaces every match in the line
ref:    change.txt *:s_g*
quote:  Replace all occurrences in the line.
given:  |a-a-a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>
expect: |b-b-b
mode:   normal

=== :s leaves the cursor on the first non-blank of the line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  When [range] and [count] are omitted, replace in the current line only.
given:
  one
      foo ba|r
  three
keys:   :s/bar/baz/<CR>
expect:
  one
      |foo baz
  three
mode:   normal

=== :%s leaves the cursor on the last substituted line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |x one
  two
  x three
  four
keys:   :%s/x/y/<CR>
expect:
  y one
  two
  |y three
  four
mode:   normal

=== :%s is undone in one step
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |x one
  x two
keys:   :%s/x/y/<CR>
expect:
  y one
  |y two
undo:
  |x one
  x two
mode:   normal

=== :s on a range
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |x
  x
  x
  x
keys:   :2,3s/x/y/<CR>
expect:
  x
  y
  |y
  x
mode:   normal

=== :s with a count replaces in that many lines
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  When [count] is given, replace in [count] lines, starting with the last line in [range].
given:
  x
  |x
  x
  x
  x
keys:   :s/x/y/ 3<CR>
expect:
  x
  y
  y
  |y
  x
mode:   normal

=== :s with a range and a count starts at the range's last line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  When [count] is given, replace in [count] lines, starting with the last line in [range].
given:
  |x
  x
  x
  x
  x
keys:   :1,2s/x/y/g 2<CR>
expect:
  x
  y
  |y
  x
  x
mode:   normal

=== more than 'report' substitutions are reported
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  For the ":substitute" command the number of substitutions is used instead of the number of lines.
given:
  |a a
  a
  b
keys:   :%s/a/x/g<CR>
expect:
  x x
  |x
  b
msg:    3 substitutions on 2 lines
mode:   normal

=== substitutions on one line are reported
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  For the ":substitute" command the number of substitutions is used instead of the number of lines.
given:  |a a a a
keys:   :s/a/x/g<CR>
expect: |x x x x
msg:    4 substitutions on 1 line
mode:   normal

=== two substitutions are not reported
ref:    options.txt *'report'*
quote:  For the ":substitute" command the number of substitutions is used instead of the number of lines.
given:  |a a
keys:   :s/a/x/g<CR>
expect: |x x
msg:    ""
mode:   normal

=== a missing pattern is an error
ref:    pattern.txt *E486*
quote:  Pattern not found
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/three/3/<CR>
expect: |one two
msg:    "E486: Pattern not found: three"
mode:   normal

=== :%s without any match is an error
ref:    pattern.txt *E486*
quote:  Pattern not found
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :%s/three/3/<CR>
expect:
  |one
  two
msg:    "E486: Pattern not found: three"
mode:   normal

=== the e flag suppresses the not-found error
ref:    change.txt *:s_e*
quote:  When the search pattern fails, do not issue an error message
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/three/3/e<CR>
expect: |one two
msg:    ""
mode:   normal

=== the i flag ignores case
ref:    change.txt *:s_i*
quote:  Ignore case for the pattern.
given:  |one FOO two
keys:   :s/foo/bar/i<CR>
expect: |one bar two
mode:   normal

=== without i the match is case sensitive
ref:    change.txt *:s_i*
quote:  Ignore case for the pattern.
given:  |one FOO foo
keys:   :s/foo/bar/<CR>
expect: |one FOO bar
mode:   normal

=== the I flag matches case even with ignorecase
ref:    change.txt *:s_I*
quote:  Don't ignore case for the pattern.  The 'ignorecase' and 'smartcase' options are not used.
given:  |FOO foo
keys:   :set ic<CR>:s/foo/bar/I<CR>
expect: |FOO bar
mode:   normal

=== the n flag counts matches without substituting
ref:    change.txt *:s_n*
quote:  Report the number of matches, do not actually substitute.
given:
  |a a
  b
  a
keys:   :%s/a/x/gn<CR>
expect:
  |a a
  b
  a
msg:    3 matches on 2 lines
mode:   normal

=== the n flag reports a single match
ref:    change.txt *:s_n*
quote:  The matches are reported as if 'report' is zero.
given:  |a b
keys:   :s/a//n<CR>
expect: |a b
msg:    1 match on 1 line
mode:   normal

=== :& repeats the last substitute without its flags
ref:    change.txt *:&*
quote:  Repeat last :substitute with same search pattern and substitute string, but without the same flags.
given:
  |a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>j:&<CR>
expect:
  b b
  |b a
mode:   normal

=== :&& keeps the flags
ref:    change.txt *:&&*
quote:  Must be the first one: Keep the flags from the previous substitute command.
given:
  |a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>j:&&<CR>
expect:
  b b
  |b b
mode:   normal

=== :s/// with the & flag keeps the flags
ref:    change.txt *:&&*
quote:  :s/this/that/&
given:
  |a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>j:s/a/c/&<CR>
expect:
  b b
  |c c
mode:   normal

=== :%& repeats on every line
ref:    change.txt *:&*
quote:  Repeat last :substitute with same search pattern and substitute string
given:
  |a a
  a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/<CR>:%&&<CR>
expect:
  b b
  b a
  |b a
mode:   normal

=== :s without a pattern repeats the last substitute
ref:    change.txt *&*
quote:  Synonym for `:s` (repeat last substitute).
given:
  |a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>j:s<CR>
expect:
  b b
  |b a
mode:   normal

=== & in normal mode repeats the last substitute
ref:    change.txt *&*
quote:  Synonym for `:s` (repeat last substitute).
given:
  |a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>j&
expect:
  b b
  |b a
mode:   normal

=== g& repeats the last substitute on all lines with its flags
ref:    change.txt *g&*
quote:  Synonym for `:%s//~/&` (repeat last substitute with last search pattern on all lines with the same flags).
given:
  |a a
  a a
  a a
keys:   :s/a/b/g<CR>g&
expect:
  b b
  b b
  |b b
mode:   normal

=== an empty pattern uses the last search pattern
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  If the {pattern} for the substitute command is empty, the command uses the pattern from the last substitute or `:global` command.  If there is none, but there is a previous search pattern, that one is used.
given:  |one two three
keys:   /two<CR>:s//2/<CR>
expect: |one 2 three
mode:   normal

=== an empty pattern after a substitute and a search uses the search
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  For `:s` with an argument this already happens
given:  |blue green
keys:   :s/blue/red/<CR>/green<CR>:s//red/<CR>
expect: |red red
mode:   normal

=== :& after a search still uses the substitute pattern
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  The last command will replace "blue" with "red".
given:
  |blue green
  blue green
keys:   :s/blue/red/<CR>j/green<CR>:&<CR>
expect:
  red green
  |red green
mode:   normal

=== :~ uses the last search pattern
ref:    change.txt *:~*
quote:  Repeat last substitute with same substitute string but with last used search pattern.
given:
  |blue green
  blue green
keys:   :s/blue/red/<CR>j/green<CR>:~<CR>
expect:
  red green
  |blue red
mode:   normal

=== :&r works like :~
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  `:&r` works the same way as `:~`
given:
  |blue green
  blue green
keys:   :s/blue/red/<CR>j/green<CR>:&r<CR>
expect:
  red green
  |blue red
mode:   normal

=== an omitted string deletes the match
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  If the {string} is omitted the substitute is done as if it's empty.  Thus the matched pattern is deleted.
given:  |one TESTING two
keys:   :s/TESTING<CR>
expect: |one  two
mode:   normal

=== the closing delimiter may be left out
ref:    change.txt *:s_r*
quote:  If [flags] are omitted the separator after {string} may also be left out.
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/one/1<CR>
expect: |1 two
mode:   normal

=== another delimiter allows / in the pattern
ref:    change.txt *pattern-delimiter*
quote:  This is useful if you want to include a '/' in the search pattern or replacement string.
given:  |a/b/c
keys:   :s+/+-+g<CR>
expect: |a-b-c
mode:   normal

=== an escaped delimiter is literal
ref:    change.txt *pattern-delimiter*
quote:  This is useful if you want to include a '/' in the search pattern or replacement string.
given:  |a-b
keys:   :s/-/\//<CR>
expect: |a/b
mode:   normal

=== & in the string is the whole match
ref:    change.txt *s/\&*
quote:  replaced with the whole matched pattern
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/two/[&]/<CR>
expect: |one \[two\]
mode:   normal

=== \& in the string is a literal &
ref:    change.txt *s/\&*
quote:  replaced with &
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/two/\&/<CR>
expect: |one &
mode:   normal

=== \0 is the whole match
ref:    change.txt *s/\0*
quote:  replaced with the whole matched pattern
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/two/<\0>/<CR>
expect: |one <two>
mode:   normal

=== \1 and \2 are the groups
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  :s/\([abc]\)\([efg]\)/\2\1/g	 modifies "af fa bg" to "fa fa gb"
given:  |af fa bg
keys:   :s/\([abc]\)\([efg]\)/\2\1/g<CR>
expect: |fa fa gb
mode:   normal

=== an unmatched group is empty
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  either the first or second pattern in parentheses did not match, so either \1 or \2 is empty.
given:  |ab
keys:   :s/\(a\)\(x*\)b/[\1\2]/<CR>
expect: |\[a\]
mode:   normal

=== ~ is the previous replacement string
ref:    change.txt *s~*
quote:  substitute
given:
  |a b
  a b
keys:   :s/a/X/<CR>j:s/b/~Y/<CR>
expect:
  X b
  |a XY
mode:   normal

=== \~ is a literal tilde
ref:    change.txt *s/\~*
quote:  replaced with ~
given:
  |a b
  a b
keys:   :s/a/X/<CR>j:s/b/\~/<CR>
expect:
  X b
  |a ~
mode:   normal

=== \u uppercases the next character
ref:    change.txt *s/\u*
quote:  next character made uppercase
given:  |bla bla
keys:   :s/bla/\u&/g<CR>
expect: |Bla Bla
mode:   normal

=== \U uppercases until \E
ref:    change.txt *s/\U*
quote:  following characters made uppercase, until \E
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/one/\U&\E!x/<CR>
expect: |ONE!x two
mode:   normal

=== \U runs to the end of the string without \E
ref:    change.txt *s/\U*
quote:  following characters made uppercase, until \E
given:  |one two
keys:   :s/one/\U&x/<CR>
expect: |ONEX two
mode:   normal

=== \l lowercases the next character
ref:    change.txt *s/\l*
quote:  next character made lowercase
given:  |ONE TWO
keys:   :s/ONE/\l&/<CR>
expect: |oNE TWO
mode:   normal

=== \L lowercases until \e
ref:    change.txt *s/\e*
quote:  end of \u, \U, \l and \L (NOTE: not <Esc>!)
given:  |ONE TWO
keys:   :s/ONE/\L&\eX/<CR>
expect: |oneX TWO
mode:   normal

=== \L\u capitalises a word
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  :s/\w\+/\L\u\0/g		 modifies "BLA bla"  to "Bla Bla"
given:  |BLA bla
keys:   :s/\w\+/\L\u\0/g<CR>
expect: |Bla Bla
mode:   normal

=== \r splits the line
ref:    change.txt *s/\r*
quote:  \r	  idem
given:  |one,two
keys:   :s/,/\r/<CR>
expect:
  one
  |two
mode:   normal

=== \r on several lines splits each
ref:    change.txt *s/\r*
quote:  \r	  idem
given:
  |a,b
  c,d
keys:   :%s/,/\r/<CR>
expect:
  a
  b
  c
  |d
mode:   normal

=== \\ is a single backslash
ref:    change.txt *s/\\*
quote:  insert a single backslash
given:  |a b
keys:   :s/ /\\/<CR>
expect: |a\\b
mode:   normal

=== \n in the pattern joins lines
ref:    pattern.txt */\n*
quote:  matches an end-of-line
given:
  |one
  two
  three
keys:   :s/e\nt/e-t/<CR>
expect:
  |one-two
  three
mode:   normal

=== :%s with \n joins every line pair it matches
ref:    pattern.txt */\n*
quote:  matches an end-of-line
given:
  |a
  b
  c
  d
keys:   :%s/\n/,/<CR>
expect: |a,b,c,d,
mode:   normal

=== ^ inserts at the start of each line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :%s/^/> /<CR>
expect:
  > one
  |> two
mode:   normal

=== $ appends at the end of each line
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |one
  two
keys:   :%s/$/;/<CR>
expect:
  one;
  |two;
mode:   normal

=== an empty match with g matches before every character
ref:    change.txt *:s_g*
quote:  Replace all occurrences in the line.
given:  |abc
keys:   :s/x*/-/g<CR>
expect: |-a-b-c
mode:   normal

=== an empty line is matched by ^$
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For each line in [range] replace a match of {pattern} with {string}.
given:
  |one

  two
keys:   :%s/^$/--/<CR>
expect:
  one
  |--
  two
mode:   normal

=== word boundaries limit the match
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For the {pattern} see |pattern|.
given:  |a ab ba a
keys:   :s/\<a\>/X/g<CR>
expect: |X ab ba X
mode:   normal

=== . and * in the pattern
ref:    change.txt *:s*
quote:  For the {pattern} see |pattern|.
given:  |key = value
keys:   :s/=.*/= x/<CR>
expect: |key = x
mode:   normal

=== :s on the visual area
ref:    cmdline.txt *v_:*
quote:  Starts a command-line with the Visual selected lines as a range.
given:
  |a
  a
  a
keys:   Vj:s/a/b/<CR>
expect:
  b
  |b
  a
mode:   normal

=== a zero count is an error
ref:    cmdline.txt *:range-pattern*
quote:  The count cannot be negative.
given:  |a
keys:   :s/a/b/ 0<CR>
expect: |a
msg:    "E939: Positive count required"
mode:   normal
